{"subjectId":"c","id":"input-output","title":"Input & Output: printf and scanf","group":"Getting Started","order":4,"html":"<p class=\"lead\"><code>printf</code> formats values into text on <code>stdout</code>; <code>scanf</code> parses text from <code>stdin</code> into variables. Both are driven by <em>format specifiers</em> that must match the argument types exactly.</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-list\"></i>Format specifiers</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Specifier</th><th>Type (printf)</th><th>Type (scanf)</th><th>Example output</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>%d</code> / <code>%i</code></td><td><code>int</code></td><td><code>int *</code></td><td><code>-42</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>%u</code></td><td><code>unsigned int</code></td><td><code>unsigned *</code></td><td><code>42</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>%ld</code> / <code>%lld</code></td><td><code>long</code> / <code>long long</code></td><td>pointer to same</td><td><code>9000000000</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>%zu</code></td><td><code>size_t</code></td><td><code>size_t *</code></td><td><code>8</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>%f</code></td><td><code>double</code> (and <code>float</code>, promoted)</td><td><code>float *</code></td><td><code>3.140000</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>%lf</code></td><td><code>double</code></td><td><code>double *</code> (<strong>required</strong>)</td><td><code>3.140000</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>%e</code> / <code>%g</code></td><td><code>double</code> scientific / shortest</td><td>same as <code>%f</code></td><td><code>3.14e+00</code> / <code>3.14</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>%c</code></td><td><code>int</code> (a char)</td><td><code>char *</code></td><td><code>A</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>%s</code></td><td><code>char *</code> (NUL-terminated)</td><td><code>char *</code> (array)</td><td><code>hello</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>%x</code> / <code>%X</code> / <code>%o</code></td><td><code>unsigned</code> hex / HEX / octal</td><td><code>unsigned *</code></td><td><code>ff</code> / <code>FF</code> / <code>377</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>%p</code></td><td><code>void *</code></td><td><code>void **</code></td><td><code>0x16d…</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>%%</code></td><td>a literal <code>%</code></td><td>—</td><td><code>%</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-adjustments\"></i>Width, precision and flags</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"c\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\nint main(void) {\n    int n = 42;\n    double pi = 3.14159265;\n    printf(\"[%d] [%5d] [%-5d] [%05d] [%+d]\\n\", n, n, n, n, n);\n    printf(\"[%f] [%.2f] [%8.3f] [%e] [%g]\\n\", pi, pi, pi, pi, pi);\n    printf(\"[%x] [%X] [%#x] [%o] [%#o]\\n\", 255, 255, 255, 8, 8);\n    printf(\"[%s] [%10s] [%-10s] [%.3s]\\n\", \"hello\", \"hi\", \"hi\", \"truncate\");\n    printf(\"[%*d] [%.*f]\\n\", 6, n, 1, pi);    /* width / precision from args */\n    printf(\"100%% done, char %c, code %d\\n\", 'A', 'A');\n    return 0;\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>[42] [   42] [42   ] [00042] [+42]\n[3.141593] [3.14] [   3.142] [3.141593e+00] [3.14159]\n[ff] [FF] [0xff] [10] [010]\n[hello] [        hi] [hi        ] [tru]\n[    42] [3.1]\n100% done, char A, code 65</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p>Format: <code>%[flags][width][.precision][length]specifier</code>. Flags: <code>-</code> left-align, <code>+</code> show sign, <code>0</code> zero-pad, <code>#</code> alternate form (<code>0x</code>), space = space for positive numbers.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-keyboard\"></i>Reading input with scanf</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"c\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\nint main(void) {\n    int age;\n    double height;\n    char name[20];\n\n    /* scanf needs ADDRESSES: &amp;age, &amp;height — but an array name already is one */\n    int got = scanf(\"%19s %d %lf\", name, &amp;age, &amp;height);\n    printf(\"read %d items: %s is %d years, %.2f m\\n\", got, name, age, height);\n    return 0;\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>read 3 items: Asha is 21 years, 1.68 m</code></pre></figure>\n<p><code>scanf</code> returns the number of items successfully converted (or <code>EOF</code>). Always check it — on bad input the variables are left untouched:</p>\n<figure data-lang=\"c\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\nint main(void) {\n    int x = -1;\n    if (scanf(\"%d\", &amp;x) != 1) {\n        printf(\"not a number, x still %d\\n\", x);\n    } else {\n        printf(\"x = %d\\n\", x);\n    }\n    return 0;\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>not a number, x still -1</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-text-wrap\"></i>Reading a whole line: fgets</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"c\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n#include &lt;string.h&gt;\n\nint main(void) {\n    char line[64];\n    while (fgets(line, sizeof line, stdin) != NULL) {\n        line[strcspn(line, \"\\n\")] = '\\0';   /* strip the trailing newline */\n        printf(\"got \\\"%s\\\" (%zu chars)\\n\", line, strlen(line));\n    }\n    return 0;\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>got \"hello world\" (11 chars)\ngot \"second line\" (11 chars)</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-abc\"></i>Character I/O</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"c\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n#include &lt;ctype.h&gt;\n\nint main(void) {\n    int c;                         /* int, not char: must hold EOF (-1) too */\n    while ((c = getchar()) != EOF) {\n        putchar(toupper(c));\n    }\n    return 0;\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>SHOUT THIS</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-alert-triangle\"></i>Pitfalls</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Mistake</th><th>Consequence</th><th>Fix</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>scanf(\"%d\", x)</code> — missing <code>&amp;</code></td><td>Writes to a garbage address → crash</td><td><code>scanf(\"%d\", &amp;x)</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>scanf(\"%s\", buf)</code> with no width</td><td>Buffer overflow on long input</td><td><code>scanf(\"%19s\", buf)</code> for <code>char buf[20]</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>%f</code> to read a <code>double</code></td><td>Writes only 4 bytes → wrong value</td><td>Use <code>%lf</code> in <code>scanf</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>gets(buf)</code></td><td>Unbounded — removed in C11</td><td><code>fgets(buf, sizeof buf, stdin)</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>scanf(\"%d\")</code> then <code>fgets</code></td><td><code>fgets</code> reads the leftover <code>\\n</code> → empty line</td><td>Consume the rest of the line first</td></tr><tr><td><code>printf(user_input)</code></td><td>Format-string vulnerability</td><td><code>printf(\"%s\", user_input)</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p><code>stdout</code> is <em>line-buffered</em> on a terminal but <em>fully buffered</em> when redirected to a file or pipe. Output may appear later than you expect — use <code>fflush(stdout)</code> or print to <code>stderr</code> (unbuffered) for diagnostics.</p></div>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Why is getchar's result stored in an int?</summary><div><p><code>getchar</code> returns every possible <code>unsigned char</code> value (0–255) <em>plus</em> <code>EOF</code> (a negative value, usually −1). A <code>char</code> cannot represent all 257 values, so <code>EOF</code> would be confused with a real character (or never detected if <code>char</code> is unsigned).</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Why does printf use %f for both float and double, but scanf needs %lf for double?</summary><div><p>Arguments to variadic functions undergo <em>default argument promotions</em>: a <code>float</code> passed to <code>printf</code> is converted to <code>double</code>, so <code>%f</code> always receives a <code>double</code>. <code>scanf</code> receives <em>pointers</em>, which are not promoted — it must know whether to write 4 bytes (<code>float *</code>, <code>%f</code>) or 8 bytes (<code>double *</code>, <code>%lf</code>).</p></div></details></section>"}